              "start": 5
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Petitioner counsel Patrick Strawbridge argued that the subpoenas are unprecedented and that no court has ever upheld Congress\u2019s power to demand a sitting President\u2019s personal records."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Strawbridge",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "91c0b544df5566ac5f3a98e133ae7e598aef0753b4751fbdd5b1830109360769",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-91c0b544df55-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 27,
              "start": 27
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Strawbridge, strawbridge also asserted that the subpoena power is an implied power and that Congress cannot use it to challenge the structure of government, citing NFIB v. Sebelius."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Letter",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "91c0b544df5566ac5f3a98e133ae7e598aef0753b4751fbdd5b1830109360769",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-91c0b544df55-0004-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 86,
              "start": 86
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Mr. Letter noted that no claim of privilege or burden had been made regarding the subpoena."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Patrick Strawbridge",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "91c0b544df5566ac5f3a98e133ae7e598aef0753b4751fbdd5b1830109360769",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-91c0b544df55-0004-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 95,
              "start": 95
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Patrick Strawbridge, strawbridge suggested that even the President\u2019s grandchildren\u2019s medical records could be subject to subpoena under the same theory."
      }
    ],
